1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,455
Every tool on this computer needs a model.

2
00:00:03,455 --> 00:00:11,516
Each one used to carry its own address, its own key, and its own plan for when the internet drops.

3
00:00:11,516 --> 00:00:13,902
Fairlead is the model router.

4
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:19,331
One local address, one key per tool, and a trace of every request.

5
00:00:19,331 --> 00:00:21,151
Here is how Fairlead fits together.

6
00:00:21,151 --> 00:00:28,379
Fleet tools call one address on this computer, in the OpenAI or the Anthropic dialect, each with its own key.

7
00:00:28,905 --> 00:00:36,266
Inside the engine, a request is checked, routed, retried if a target fails, and logged.

8
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:40,419
The log keeps the decision, never the prompt.

9
00:00:41,119 --> 00:00:44,055
One piece makes the outbound calls.

10
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:47,763
Local models, Ollama and Windlass.

11
00:00:47,763 --> 00:00:51,085
Cloud providers, OpenAI and OpenRouter.

12
00:00:51,695 --> 00:00:57,296
Cloud keys live in Lazarette, shared by name, and held in memory only.

13
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,700
Mark a request private, and it never leaves this PC.

14
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:09,468
Planned, not built: git hosts and databases behind the same address, and routing by prompt difficulty.

15
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:11,578
Every request leaves a trace.

16
00:01:12,127 --> 00:01:13,060
That is the evidence.

17
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:14,040
Start with the key.

18
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:22,756
It goes into Lazarette, the encrypted vault on this machine, as an API credential named OPENAI underscore API underscore KEY.

19
00:01:23,068 --> 00:01:29,061
Test credential checks it against OpenAI's read only endpoint before anything uses it.

20
00:01:29,061 --> 00:01:31,830
This footage comes from the Halyard demo.

21
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:34,467
Then share it with Fairlead by name.

22
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:37,135
Fairlead sees only what you share.

23
00:01:37,912 --> 00:01:41,727
Fairlead reads it from the vault and connects.

24
00:01:41,727 --> 00:01:45,560
That is the only place the cloud key lives.

25
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:46,461
The Providers page.

26
00:01:46,461 --> 00:01:51,513
Lazarette is paired, and Fairlead asks it for two names, both shared.

27
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:56,699
Lazarette is locked right now, so Fairlead keeps what it already read for eight hours.

28
00:01:57,399 --> 00:01:58,199
OpenAI.

29
00:01:57,755 --> 00:01:59,269
Test connection.

30
00:01:59,269 --> 00:02:04,035
Connected, with the key from Lazarette, not typed here.

31
00:02:04,735 --> 00:02:06,817
Ollama, the local provider.

32
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:14,190
Fetch model list finds Llama three point two, three billion, imported from a model file already on this PC.

33
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:17,090
Routes are the model names your tools send.

34
00:02:17,090 --> 00:02:24,220
Smart is one target, fast is another, and each can hold an ordered list that fails over to the next.

35
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:30,855
The local route was empty, so we give it a target: the model we just installed.

36
00:02:30,855 --> 00:02:33,095
Private requests and offline mode land here.

37
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:44,413
Auto picks a route for each request from rules: prompt size, tools, images, keywords, and whether the prompt carries a secret.

38
00:02:45,113 --> 00:02:50,639
Aliases catch hard coded names, so any claude or gpt name lands on a route.

39
00:02:50,639 --> 00:02:52,070
Every tool gets its own key.

40
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:59,971
The Install button makes one and saves it to Windows Credential Manager, so the tool reads it from there and it never sits in a config file.

41
00:03:00,671 --> 00:03:02,952
Keys are shown once and stored only as a hash.

42
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:10,316
Each can be limited by route, by rate, and by daily tokens, and Revoke ends one tool without touching the rest.

43
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:18,178
Each tool's setup is copy and paste: the OpenAI SDK, curl with the privacy header, or the Halyard profile.

44
00:03:18,178 --> 00:03:20,981
Claude Code needs just a base URL and a token.

45
00:03:21,231 --> 00:03:22,031
Settings.

46
00:03:21,704 --> 00:03:24,140
Kedge mode is the offline switch.

47
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,772
Auto goes local the moment the cloud is out of reach.

48
00:03:27,772 --> 00:03:34,002
I will not force it on here, because other tools share this Fairlead right now.

49
00:03:34,702 --> 00:03:41,616
Privacy: look for secrets in prompts, keep private requests on local models, require a client key.

50
00:03:41,616 --> 00:03:46,790
Prompts are kept only if you tick content logging, in memory, never on disk.

51
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:48,188
It is off.

52
00:03:48,888 --> 00:03:49,688
Diagnostics.

53
00:03:49,472 --> 00:03:57,709
The network, and each provider: Ollama and OpenAI reachable, Windlass not running, with the reason.

54
00:03:58,409 --> 00:04:05,820
Circuit breakers below keep dead targets out of the way, so a failing model stops costing every request a retry.

55
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:06,983
A real request.

56
00:04:06,952 --> 00:04:09,036
Model auto, a short prompt.

57
00:04:09,036 --> 00:04:15,901
Explain shows the plan without calling a model: the quick chat rule matched, and the first choice is OpenAI mini.

58
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:17,401
Send.

59
00:04:16,865 --> 00:04:21,661
OpenAI answered, and the decision and the attempt are right there.

60
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:25,044
Now a prompt that carries a cloud key.

61
00:04:25,044 --> 00:04:30,027
It is the example key from Amazon's documentation, not a real credential.

62
00:04:30,027 --> 00:04:34,244
Explain says private request, local models only.

63
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:36,584
Send.

64
00:04:37,284 --> 00:04:38,090
It never leaves this PC.

65
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:40,177
Llama answered, and the log marks it private.

66
00:04:40,877 --> 00:04:45,241
Without a secret, tick private, which sends the privacy header any tool can set.

67
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:48,039
Same result: it stays on this machine.

68
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:49,936
Now another Fleet tool.

69
00:04:49,936 --> 00:04:53,193
This is Halyard, with its own Fairlead key.

70
00:04:53,193 --> 00:04:56,414
Test model sends a real request through Fairlead.

71
00:04:56,414 --> 00:04:59,543
Reply from auto, in under half a second.

72
00:05:00,243 --> 00:05:04,439
On the overview, the routing map shows each tool as a client, and the providers it reached.

73
00:05:05,139 --> 00:05:08,419
Activity lists every request with its client, route and target.

74
00:05:08,419 --> 00:05:12,651
Halyard's is right there, next to other demos running on this PC.

75
00:05:12,651 --> 00:05:17,199
Open one: the rule that matched, the target and the timing.

76
00:05:17,199 --> 00:05:18,723
Prompts are not kept.

77
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:20,592
Failover is logged too.

78
00:05:20,592 --> 00:05:26,367
I sent this one from the command line, to a route whose first model was retired.

79
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:32,469
OpenAI answered not found, and Fairlead moved to the next target before any byte reached the caller.

80
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,886
Fairlead is the one address.

81
00:05:34,886 --> 00:05:44,314
Keys stay in the vault, each tool holds one key, private requests stay on this PC, and every request leaves a trace you can read.

82
00:05:44,314 --> 00:05:48,627
The demos in this series that call a model went through it.

83
00:05:48,627 --> 00:05:53,158
Planned next: git hosts and databases behind the same address.

84
00:05:53,158 --> 00:05:54,401
That's the watch.
